  "claims": [
    {
      "id": "c.setup",
      "kind": "framing",
      "text": "main calls factorial(3), and factorial calls itself with n - 1 until n is 1.",
      "justification": "factorial.c line 13 calls factorial(3); line 7 calls factorial(n - 1) only while n > 1 (line 6).",
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "programs/factorial.c",
          "url": "programs/factorial.c",
          "clause": "lines 3-15"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c.frame",
      "kind": "rule",
      "text": "Each call to factorial gets its own n and its own r. This lesson draws each call to factorial as a frame on the call stack.",
      "justification": "6.2.4p6: an automatic object's lifetime is from entry into its block until the block ends, and if the block is entered recursively a new instance is created each time; n (a parameter) and r are automatic objects of factorial's body, so every active call has its own. C does not name a stack; the frame picture is this lesson's drawing of those separate instances, kept in call order.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "ISO/IEC 9899:2018 (C17), working draft N2176",
          "url": "https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg14/www/docs/n2176.pdf",
          "clause": "§6.2.4 ¶6",
          "quote": "If the block is entered recursively, a new instance of the object is created each time."
        },
        {
          "title": "ISO/IEC 9899:2018 (C17), working draft N2176",
          "url": "https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg14/www/docs/n2176.pdf",
          "clause": "§6.5.2.2 ¶11",
          "quote": "Recursive function calls shall be permitted, both directly and indirectly through any chain of other functions."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c.waits",
      "kind": "mechanism",
      "text": "factorial(3) cannot finish line 7 until factorial(2) returns a value, so its frame waits under the new one.",
      "justification": "Line 7 computes n * factorial(n - 1); the value of a function call expression is the value its return statement returns (6.5.2.2p5, 6.8.6.4p3), so the multiplication and everything after it in factorial(3) happen only after factorial(2) returns. Meanwhile factorial(3)'s own n and r still exist (6.2.4p6). The run printed enter factorial(3) then enter factorial(2) before any result line.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "ISO/IEC 9899:2018 (C17), working draft N2176",
          "url": "https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg14/www/docs/n2176.pdf",
          "clause": "§6.8.6.4 ¶3",
          "quote": "If a return statement with an expression is executed, the value of the expression is returned to the caller as the value of the function call expression."
        },
        {
          "title": "ISO/IEC 9899:2018 (C17), working draft N2176",
          "url": "https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg14/www/docs/n2176.pdf",
